concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-58 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "58 Use of electronic equipment at premises"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-58 ;
    values:originalText "(1) The executing officer or an officer assisting may operate electronic equipment at the warrant premises to see whether evidential material is accessible by doing so if he or she believes on reasonable grounds that the operation of the equipment can be carried out without damage to the equipment. (2) If the executing officer or an officer assisting, after operating the equipment, finds that evidential material is accessible by doing so, he or she may: (a) seize the equipment and any disk, tape or other associated device; or   Search, seizure and powers of arrest  Part 7 Provisions relating to execution of search warrants  Division 2 Section 58 International War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995 Compilation date: 28/03/2026 (b) if the material can, by using facilities at the premises, be put in a documentary form-operate the facilities to put the material in that form and seize the documents so produced; or (c) if the material can be transferred to a disk, tape or other storage device: (i) that is brought to the premises; or (ii) that is at the premises and the use of which for the purpose has been agreed to in writing by the occupier of the premises; operate the equipment or other facilities to copy the material to the storage device and take the storage device from the premises. (3) Equipment may be seized under paragraph (2)(a) only if: (a) it is not practicable to put the material in documentary form as mentioned in paragraph (2)(b) or to copy the material as mentioned in paragraph (2)(c); or (b) possession by the occupier of the equipment could constitute an offence against an Australian law. (4) If the executing officer or an officer assisting believes on reasonable grounds that: (a) evidential material may be accessible by operating electronic equipment at the warrant premises; and (b) expert assistance is required to operate the equipment; and (c) if he or she does not take action under this subsection, the material may be destroyed, altered or otherwise interfered with; he or she may do whatever is necessary to secure the equipment, whether by locking it up, placing a guard or otherwise. (5) The executing officer or an officer assisting must give notice to the occupier of the premises of his or her intention to secure equipment and of the fact that the equipment may be secured for up to 24 hours. (6) The equipment may be secured:" ;
    skos:definition "(1) The executing officer or an officer assisting may operate electronic equipment at the warrant premises to see whether evidential material is accessible by doing so if he or she believes on reasonable grounds that the operation of the equipment can be carried out without damage to the equipment. (2) If the executing officer or an officer assisting, after operating the equipment, finds that evidential material is accessible by doing so, he or she may: (a) seize the equipment and any disk, tape or other associated device; or   Search, seizure and powers of arrest  Part 7 Provisions relating to execution of search warrants  Division 2 Section 58 International War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995 Compilation date: 28/03/2026 (b) if the material can, by using facilities at the premises, be put in a documentary form-operate the facilities to put the material in that form and seize the documents so produced; or (c) if the material can be transferred to a disk, tape or other storage device: (i) that is brought to the premises; or (ii) that is at the premises and the use of which for the purpose has been agreed to in writing by the occupier of the premises; operate the equipment or other facilities to copy the material to the storage device and take the storage device from the premises. (3) Equipment may be seized under paragraph (2)(a) only if: (a) it is not practicable to put the material in documentary form as mentioned in paragraph (2)(b) or to copy the material as mentioned in paragraph (2)(c); or (b) possession by the occupier of the equipment could constitute an offence against an Australian law. (4) If the executing officer or an officer assisting believes on reasonable grounds that: (a) evidential material may be accessible by operating electronic equipment at the warrant premises; and (b) expert assistance is required to operate the equipment; and (c) if he or she does not take action under this subsection, the material may be destroyed, altered or otherwise interfered"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "5bf96b66d5e3bbecfba043308bcc674e4c6bef5228a2ac65be373a40c25fef18" ;
    cof:pageNumber "82"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-58-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-58-ss-2 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-58-ss-3 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-58-ss-4 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-58-ss-5 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-58-ss-6 ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
    cml:schemaVersion "2" ;
    cml:proposedBy <https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf> ;
    dc:source "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf, p.82" .
doc:sec-58 a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "58 Use of electronic equipment at premises" ;
    values:kind "section" ;
    values:originalText "(1) The executing officer or an officer assisting may operate electronic equipment at the warrant premises to see whether evidential material is accessible by doing so if he or she believes on reasonable grounds that the operation of the equipment can be carried out without damage to the equipment. (2) If the executing officer or an officer assisting, after operating the equipment, finds that evidential material is accessible by doing so, he or she may: (a) seize the equipment and any disk, tape or other associated device; or   Search, seizure and powers of arrest  Part 7 Provisions relating to execution of search warrants  Division 2 Section 58 International War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995 Compilation date: 28/03/2026 (b) if the material can, by using facilities at the premises, be put in a documentary form-operate the facilities to put the material in that form and seize the documents so produced; or (c) if the material can be transferred to a disk, tape or other storage device: (i) that is brought to the premises; or (ii) that is at the premises and the use of which for the purpose has been agreed to in writing by the occupier of the premises; operate the equipment or other facilities to copy the material to the storage device and take the storage device from the premises. (3) Equipment may be seized under paragraph (2)(a) only if: (a) it is not practicable to put the material in documentary form as mentioned in paragraph (2)(b) or to copy the material as mentioned in paragraph (2)(c); or (b) possession by the occupier of the equipment could constitute an offence against an Australian law. (4) If the executing officer or an officer assisting believes on reasonable grounds that: (a) evidential material may be accessible by operating electronic equipment at the warrant premises; and (b) expert assistance is required to operate the equipment; and (c) if he or she does not take action under this subsection, the material may be destroyed, altered or otherwise interfered with; he or she may do whatever is necessary to secure the equipment, whether by locking it up, placing a guard or otherwise. (5) The executing officer or an officer assisting must give notice to the occupier of the premises of his or her intention to secure equipment and of the fact that the equipment may be secured for up to 24 hours. (6) The equipment may be secured:" ;
    cof:pageNumber "82"^^xsd:integer .

