concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-63 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "63 Searches without warrant in emergency situations"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-63 ;
    values:originalText "(1) This section applies if a police officer suspects, on reasonable grounds, that: (a) evidential material is in or on a conveyance; and (b) it is necessary to exercise a power under subsection (2) in order to prevent the material from being concealed, lost or destroyed; and (c) it is necessary to exercise the power without the authority of a search warrant because the circumstances are serious and urgent. (2) The police officer may: (a) stop and detain the conveyance; and (b) search the conveyance, and any container in or on the conveyance, for the material; and (c) seize the material if he or she finds it there. (3) If, in the course of searching for the material, the police officer finds other evidential material or a thing relevant to an offence against an Australian law, the police officer may seize that material or thing if he or she suspects, on reasonable grounds, that: (a) it is necessary to seize it in order to prevent its concealment, loss or destruction; and (b) it is necessary to seize it without the authority of a search warrant because the circumstances are serious and urgent. (4) The police officer must exercise his or her powers subject to section 64." ;
    skos:definition "(1) This section applies if a police officer suspects, on reasonable grounds, that: (a) evidential material is in or on a conveyance; and (b) it is necessary to exercise a power under subsection (2) in order to prevent the material from being concealed, lost or destroyed; and (c) it is necessary to exercise the power without the authority of a search warrant because the circumstances are serious and urgent. (2) The police officer may: (a) stop and detain the conveyance; and (b) search the conveyance, and any container in or on the conveyance, for the material; and (c) seize the material if he or she finds it there. (3) If, in the course of searching for the material, the police officer finds other evidential material or a thing relevant to an offence against an Australian law, the police officer may seize that material or thing if he or she suspects, on reasonable grounds, that: (a) it is necessary to seize it in order to prevent its concealment, loss or destruction; and (b) it is necessary to seize it without the authority of a search warrant because the circumstances are serious and urgent. (4) The police officer must exercise his or her powers subject to section 64."@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "bd614206580f010218225a731a984c31532f7a6db13f17f4cdf282167efee2a3" ;
    cof:pageNumber "87"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-63-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-63-ss-2 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-63-ss-3 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-63-ss-4 ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
    cml:schemaVersion "2" ;
    cml:proposedBy <https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf> ;
    dc:source "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf, p.87" .
doc:sec-63 a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "63 Searches without warrant in emergency situations" ;
    values:kind "section" ;
    values:originalText "(1) This section applies if a police officer suspects, on reasonable grounds, that: (a) evidential material is in or on a conveyance; and (b) it is necessary to exercise a power under subsection (2) in order to prevent the material from being concealed, lost or destroyed; and (c) it is necessary to exercise the power without the authority of a search warrant because the circumstances are serious and urgent. (2) The police officer may: (a) stop and detain the conveyance; and (b) search the conveyance, and any container in or on the conveyance, for the material; and (c) seize the material if he or she finds it there. (3) If, in the course of searching for the material, the police officer finds other evidential material or a thing relevant to an offence against an Australian law, the police officer may seize that material or thing if he or she suspects, on reasonable grounds, that: (a) it is necessary to seize it in order to prevent its concealment, loss or destruction; and (b) it is necessary to seize it without the authority of a search warrant because the circumstances are serious and urgent. (4) The police officer must exercise his or her powers subject to section 64." ;
    cof:pageNumber "87"^^xsd:integer .

concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-63-ss-1 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "63(1) Searches without warrant in emergency situations"@en ;
